            <ArticleTitle>Resisting the Urge to Act: DREADDs Modifying Habits.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>61-62</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0166-2236(16)30179-5</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.tins.2016.12.002</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Recently, Meyer and Bucci used chemogenetic technology - artificial excitatory and inhibitory receptors - to modulate neuronal activity in two connected brain regions in opposite directions simultaneously. This innovative manipulation revealed that the two regions studied, orbitofrontal cortex and nucleus accumbens, are not sequentially dependent during contextual decision-making.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Published by Elsevier Ltd.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
